Your GP should refer you to an **eating disorder service**. In some areas you can refer yourself. You should have regular **physical health checks**, such as weight, blood tests and heart checks, as well as mental health support.

## Anorexia

- **Adults:** a talking therapy such as **CBT for eating disorders (CBT-ED)**, **MANTRA** (Maudsley Anorexia Nervosa Treatment for Adults) or **SSCM** (specialist supportive clinical management). These help you understand your eating disorder and return to healthy eating and weight.
- **Children and young people:** **family therapy** focused on anorexia, where the whole family works together to support recovery.

## Bulimia

- **Adults:** usually **guided self-help** based on CBT first, then **CBT-ED** if needed.
- **Children and young people:** **family therapy** focused on bulimia.

## Binge eating disorder

Usually starts with **guided self-help** based on CBT. If that is not enough, you may be offered group or individual **CBT-ED**. Medicine is not usually offered for binge eating disorder alone.

## ARFID

Treatment is tailored to the reasons you avoid or restrict food. It often involves a mix of psychological help, dietetic support and, for children, family involvement.

## Hospital and day care

Some people need more intensive care, such as a **day programme** or a stay in **hospital**, if their physical health is at risk.

## Medicines

There is no medicine that treats eating disorders directly. You may be offered medicine for other conditions, such as depression or anxiety.

Next, read about [living well and recovery](/conditions/mental-health/eating-disorders/living-well/).
